WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.920
My name is John Kiriakou. I was a field&nbsp;
agent for the CIA. I hunted, tracked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:12.560
and captured terrorists around the world. This&nbsp;
is everything I'm authorized to tell you. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:17.760
not allowed to say how many officers or what&nbsp;
percentage of CIA officers are dedicated to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:23.040
counterterrorism. After 9/11, the CIA became a&nbsp;
paramilitary organization. We started borrowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:28.560
special forces guys. They would be on&nbsp;
loan to the CIA to just fly somewhere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:37.040
kill somebody, and then fly back&nbsp;
for the next Tuesday meeting.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:41.760
The most obvious risk of working as&nbsp;
a counterterrorism officer obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:48.560
is the risk of death. There were two guys who I&nbsp;
worked with in CTC, the counterterrorism center,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:55.680
Helge Boes, and Mike Spann, who were killed in&nbsp;
the line of duty. And it wasn't just them. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:01.120
was the chief of the counter intelligence branch&nbsp;
in the Osama bin Laden group. The woman that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:07.120
sat next to was killed in the line of duty in the&nbsp;
host bombing in Afghanistan. The suicide bomber,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:16.000
he blew her and six of our colleagues up. So,&nbsp;
death, destruction, but on a on a an equally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:22.160
personal note, your marriage is probably not going&nbsp;
to survive an operational tour in the director of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:30.560
intelligence. The stress is just otherworldly.&nbsp;
It's the most difficult and dangerous thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:36.960
you can do as a CIA officer for a variety of&nbsp;
reasons. First, when people join terrorist groups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:46.320
they do that because they are true believers. And&nbsp;
in most cases, they hate you or what you stand for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:54.880
more than they love life itself. And so, they are&nbsp;
very happy to die for a cause. At the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:59.760
if you're working counterterrorism issues,&nbsp;
you generally aren't doing it in London or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:08.800
Paris or Ottawa. You're doing it in Yemen,&nbsp;
Somalia, Afghanistan, Pakistan, in Pakistan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:15.200
unless you were going to carry out an operational&nbsp;
act. You had to stay on the compound. It was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:21.440
simply too dangerous. And so, not only do you have&nbsp;
this exceedingly difficult job to do, but you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:29.920
to blend in and not attract attention to yourself&nbsp;
and keep yourself safe at the same time. I'll give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:34.720
you an example. When I was stationed in Athens,&nbsp;
Athens was one of the most dangerous places in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:40.880
world for Americans, especially for American CIA&nbsp;
officers. 17 November had murdered the CIA station&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:47.600
chief, two US defense attaches, and a hapless&nbsp;
Air Force technical sergeant. This was a very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:56.080
very lethal group. And so, that was my primary job&nbsp;
to work against an incredibly deadly group. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:01.360
was the go-to guy on Arab terrorism there because&nbsp;
I spoke Arabic. And I drove an armored car,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:09.440
level four armor. I carried two guns. I had a&nbsp;
Glock 9mm on my waist and a Smith and Wesson 38 on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:14.240
my ankle. Even kept a buck knife in my back pocket&nbsp;
just in case everything turned to that's all I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:20.640
had left. Halfway through my tour, the British&nbsp;
embassy received a new defense attache. His name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:26.160
was Steven Saunders. He was a one-star general,&nbsp;
brigadier general. Everybody loved him. Everybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:31.760
liked to be around him, the life of the party.&nbsp;
He loved being on camera. So, anytime you're at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:38.160
some event and there's a camera, Steven would run&nbsp;
up to the camera and give an interview. Which is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:44.960
you know, great if you're not in a country that's&nbsp;
categorized as critical threat for terrorism. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:52.480
took delivery of this new BMW 540, fully armored.&nbsp;
And so we were at a we were at a cocktail party&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:58.000
the day that I got the car and I was standing&nbsp;
there with Stephen. He was making fun of me and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:04.960
he says, "You Americans, you're so obsessed with&nbsp;
security. It's a NATO country. What are you so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:12.160
afraid of?" And they all chuckled. And I said,&nbsp;
"You Brits live in a dream world. If you think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:15.760
because they have palm trees and pretty beaches&nbsp;
that they're not going to kill you if they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:24.960
the chance." And we all laughed again. And then&nbsp;
a couple of weeks later, I made a mistake that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:30.080
I had never made before. I slept through my&nbsp;
alarm. I had to just get on the main road and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:37.920
go straight to the embassy. And it was straight&nbsp;
down this direct road called KCS Boulevard. So I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:43.120
get on QCS to go straight down the mountain&nbsp;
and get to the embassy. Turn on the radio.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:48.960
I'm listening to some music and this announcer&nbsp;
comes on and he says, "Avoid Kifisias Boulevard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:55.440
There was a terrorist attack at Filothei." I had&nbsp;
definitely never heard that, but when he said it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.440 --> 00:05:04.400
I see this white this white rover in the far left&nbsp;
lane. There's blood all over the interior of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:08.960
windows. And I can see when I get close enough&nbsp;
that the driver side window has been shot out and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:14.400
Steven Saunders drives a white rover. It turned&nbsp;
out that they had taken him to a hospital that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:18.720
next door to the British embassy and he died there&nbsp;
about 20 minutes later. I mean, we knew who did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:24.960
it. It was revolutionary organization 17 November.&nbsp;
Now, 17 November usually would drop a manifesto at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:31.040
the site of the of the assassination or they would&nbsp;
mail one to a leftist newspaper. My boss runs into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:36.000
my office and he says, "Did you see the manifesto&nbsp;
for the Saunders hit?" He says, "It mentions you."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:42.640
I said, "Come on, not possible." Like seriously,&nbsp;
nobody took security as seriously as I did. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:48.640
he shows it to me and it says in Greek, "We saw&nbsp;
the big spy, but he was driving an armored car&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:53.440
and we knew he was armed. So we elected to&nbsp;
carry out the revolutionary sentence on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:58.800
war criminal Saunders." And he said, "You got to&nbsp;
go. We're going to take you to the airport. You're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:03.440
going to go home. We'll pick up your kids. We'll&nbsp;
pick up your wife. We'll pack at your house." So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:08.800
we flew out. The night before that happened&nbsp;
was the last night I spent in a house with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:20.320
my wife and we were divorced soon after. She's&nbsp;
just like, "Yeah, I'm not doing this anymore."

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:25.200
Assets are critically important&nbsp;
to counterterrorism because as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:29.600
American CIA officer, I'm not going to&nbsp;
be able to infiltrate a group directly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:36.400
I was one of only a handful of CIA officers who&nbsp;
spoke fluent Arabic. Even with my fluent Arabic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:43.360
I spoke with an accent. So John's not going to&nbsp;
be able to put on a thobe and a dishdasha and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:48.000
you know walk into a terrorist training camp and&nbsp;
volunteer. That's just not real life. It doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:54.240
happen. And so your job is to recruit someone&nbsp;
who is either a member of a terrorist group or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:59.440
can become a member of terrorist group so that&nbsp;
you can use his information to disrupt future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:05.440
attacks. There are a million different types of&nbsp;
people that you might want to consider recruiting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:12.000
But if you recruit a bonafide terrorist, someone&nbsp;
who is able to get into the group, you're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:19.440
to meet with him once a month for hours probably,&nbsp;
and he's going to give you five, six, 10 different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:24.000
intelligence reports. This is what they're talking&nbsp;
about. This is what they're planning. They want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:31.280
do an attack in, you know, in Bali or whatever.&nbsp;
That's the stuff that you want to get. When I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:36.640
training as a case officer, one of my instructors&nbsp;
said to me that he had gone through his entire 30&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:44.000
plus year career in operations and he never ever&nbsp;
had a target say no to a recruitment. And I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:50.640
"You must be like some kind of a magical wizard&nbsp;
of recruitment." And he laughed and he said, "No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:56.240
when you eventually say, listen, I'm not really,&nbsp;
you know, the economic officer here. I'm really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.240 --> 00:08:03.120
a CIA officer." When you drop that cover, you&nbsp;
have to be certain that they're not going to run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:08.800
screaming from the room, calling for the police,&nbsp;
reporting you to their ambassador. You're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:12.640
to get arrested, you're going to get expelled,&nbsp;
your career is going to come to a halt. You have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:19.840
to be 100% sure. That's where you take them to&nbsp;
lunch, you take them to dinner, you spend weekends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:25.440
together, you do day trips, your wives become&nbsp;
friends, your kids are playing together to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:32.240
point where you have figured out by now whether he&nbsp;
has a vulnerability. And a vulnerability can mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:40.320
a lot of different things. Maybe he just really&nbsp;
really loves his kids. That can be a vulnerability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:46.000
because then that allows me to say, "I see how&nbsp;
much you love your kids. I know you want your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:51.200
kids to go to the best schools. I can literally&nbsp;
get your kid into Harvard and I'll pay for it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:56.800
too, by the way. And he says, "Well, what do you&nbsp;
want in exchange?" Well, I want the plans to that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:02.800
new Russian tank. I'd really like to get a peek&nbsp;
at it. Another vulnerability would be, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:08.800
maybe the guy's wife has cancer, but you can get&nbsp;
her into the Mayo Clinic where she can get the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:14.800
best cancer treatment on the planet. Maybe the&nbsp;
guy's a drinker or or a gambler, even better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:21.200
and he's always short of money because he's maybe&nbsp;
embezzling a little bit from the embassy. 95% of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:26.320
recruitments are made for the money. Sometimes&nbsp;
it's ideology. Sometimes it's, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:31.440
this desire for excitement. They love James Bond.&nbsp;
They want to be a part of it. Sometimes it's for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:36.400
revenge. They hate their boss. They hate their&nbsp;
country. They hate their leader. You've got to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:43.920
develop that target to the point where he comes&nbsp;
to believe that you are his best friend. To the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:50.560
point where you know in your heart he's willing to&nbsp;
commit espionage for you. The resources at the CIA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:59.200
are virtually unlimited. In in exchange though,&nbsp;
the information has to be incredible, let's say.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:05.680
but you have access to the location of the leader&nbsp;
of al-Qaeda and we're able to break down the door&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:13.600
and grab the guy, there's a reward for you of&nbsp;
between 10 and $25 million that we will pay in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:19.920
cash, in gold, in diamonds, in land, any way you&nbsp;
want to receive it. But the budget is going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:26.320
be there if the information is good. I made a&nbsp;
recruitment one time. I heard from a source a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:33.600
group of members of a terrorist cell were meeting&nbsp;
for coffee in the same coffee house every single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:41.840
day 10:00 they'd go for coffee which is a terrible&nbsp;
thing to do by way of of trade craft right never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:48.880
never establish a pattern I started going there&nbsp;
at 9 9:30 in the morning so sure enough 10 o'clock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:54.800
they came in four or five of them and one of them&nbsp;
turned and looked at me I did that for a week.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.800 --> 00:11:00.640
I started the second week. Every day 9:30, I'm&nbsp;
sitting there with my Arabic newspaper. And this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:08.240
time, one of them nodded at me. So, I nodded back&nbsp;
to him. And then one day, he came in by himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:17.360
And he sits down and we start talking and um and I&nbsp;
ask him if how's his family? Oh, he said, "My wife&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:26.080
is in Cairo with my son and my daughter, and I've&nbsp;
never met my son." He really was a a family man.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:32.800
He was religious. He made some bad decisions and&nbsp;
ended up in Afghanistan. Then he just wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:39.280
go home. And I said to him, "If that's if that's&nbsp;
all you want, you just want to go home." I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:45.120
"Well, I can take care of that." To make a long&nbsp;
story short, I recruited him. I got him his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:50.960
passport. I gave him some money to get himself&nbsp;
established again and put him on a plane and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:59.040
he gave me in return everything I had asked for.&nbsp;
Very specific information that we used to actively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:06.320
recruit other al-Qaeda members. I asked him at the&nbsp;
end of it, why did you let me recruit you? And he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:13.680
said, I've been here for 5 years and you were the&nbsp;
first person who ever asked me about my family.

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:25.200
There are no tactics taught to help you ingratiate&nbsp;
yourself with potential targets. You either have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:31.920
it in your gut or you don't. So, a normal&nbsp;
asset meeting, you go through the mad minute.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:35.920
Were you followed here? Were you followed&nbsp;
home last time? Do you have any problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:41.520
any concerns during the course of the month?&nbsp;
and let's meet one month from tonight, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:49.600
at 8:00 at such and such a hotel. Then you sit&nbsp;
down, you have a coffee, how was your month? How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:53.600
are things going? What have you heard? What do&nbsp;
you think about this guy? What do you know about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:58.560
him? Do we need to be worried about him? Is he&nbsp;
in Is he going to be in charge of the weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:05.280
cash? That kind of thing. It's not unusual&nbsp;
for a meeting to last two or three hours.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:10.320
I was in a meeting once that lasted eight hours.&nbsp;
Could be at a safe house. It can be walking down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:16.240
the beach, anywhere where you're going to be&nbsp;
safe and that will be private. There was one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:21.360
source I recruited. It was too dangerous to meet&nbsp;
him in the country that I recruited him in. So we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:28.080
would fly to Europe and we would meet in Europe&nbsp;
once a month and we would meet like eight hours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:34.560
a day for 3 days and then I would take a train to&nbsp;
a different country so it didn't look like we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:39.760
both flying back from the same city at the same&nbsp;
time and it didn't arouse any suspicion. Yeah.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:45.760
Some meetings by necessity are conducted in cars&nbsp;
and so sometimes you're going to have to do what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.760 --> 00:13:54.000
called a CPU, a car pickup. You want to be able to&nbsp;
pick the person up at a spot whereas few people as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.000 --> 00:14:00.320
possible are going to see you. Maybe you're doing&nbsp;
it in a semi-residential area where he's standing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:05.760
on a corner. You come down an alley instead of&nbsp;
a main street. He jumps in the car and you take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:15.120
off. Let's say the meeting is at 2:00. You want&nbsp;
to be there between 158 and 202. And if you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:21.920
there at 158 and he's not there, you do one more&nbsp;
run. And if he's not there by 2002, you abort the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:27.200
meeting and you try it again 24 hours later or&nbsp;
72 hours later, whatever your agreement is. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:32.400
don't want somebody to say, "I wonder why that car&nbsp;
keeps driving in a loop, you know, and then they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.400 --> 00:14:38.880
write down your license plate number." You just&nbsp;
you don't want to come to anybody's attention.

00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:49.920
Double agents are indeed real. Double agents are a&nbsp;
very very big problem on both sides of the double.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:55.600
I was well into my career and I had a great friend&nbsp;
who was a station chief. He called me one day. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.600 --> 00:15:00.800
was at headquarters and he called me from overseas&nbsp;
and he said, "Hey, I was I was hoping you could do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:08.160
me a favor. One of my guys has recruited a double&nbsp;
agent. The double doesn't know that we know he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:16.480
a double. The enemy country has instructed&nbsp;
the double to demand to only speak with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:21.360
station chief. And he said, "I live here. It's too&nbsp;
dangerous for me to meet with this guy. Can you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:27.200
fly out here and pretend to be me and handle the&nbsp;
double agent?" I said, "Of course." So, I fly out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.200 --> 00:15:33.040
and I meet with the guy. I meet him in some hotel&nbsp;
and I give him a big bear hug at first, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.040 --> 00:15:38.480
unusually warm in the Middle East, but I needed&nbsp;
to pat him down for weapons and he didn't have any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:44.240
weapons. And so I made a big deal of taking off my&nbsp;
jacket to show him that I didn't have any weapons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:49.040
I sat down, we ordered some coffee from room&nbsp;
service. He said, "Are you the chief?" I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:54.640
"You asked for him. You got him, buddy." "I'm&nbsp;
the chief." I would meet with him once a month,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.640 --> 00:16:02.240
and this went on for about six months. We were&nbsp;
getting, you know, the take on everything that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.240 --> 00:16:07.280
was reporting back and what the enemy country was&nbsp;
telling him to do. Finally, I'm at headquarters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:13.440
one day. I get a call from a sister agency. And&nbsp;
the woman says, "Are you handling this double&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:19.120
case?" And I said, "Yeah, listen. He's been&nbsp;
instructed to kill you in the next meeting."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:23.280
I said, "Oh, come on." I said, "The guy's afraid&nbsp;
of his shadow. He's not going to kill me. I know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.280 --> 00:16:30.640
him well. He's a coward. He's not going to pull&nbsp;
a gun and shoot me. But even if he were to pull a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.640 --> 00:16:36.880
gun and shoot me, how about this? Usually he calls&nbsp;
me when he's coming into the lobby of the hotel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:42.000
I'll tell him to come up to the room directly, and&nbsp;
when he comes in, I'll prop the door open. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:46.560
he comes in, I'll grab him, take him down, you&nbsp;
guys burst in from the other from the other room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:52.240
and we get him. They reluctantly agreed with a&nbsp;
proviso that I wear a bulletproof vest. I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.240 --> 00:17:02.880
"Fine." So, I fly back out and four armed security&nbsp;
guys in the lobby and it turns out the bad guys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.880 --> 00:17:08.240
have four armed security people in the lobby and&nbsp;
they're all kind of looking at each other sideways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:13.440
like who are these guys? What's that bulge on that&nbsp;
guy's waist? That kind of thing. And they're all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.440 --> 00:17:19.520
just kind of standing around staring at each&nbsp;
other. So, he calls me the double and I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.520 --> 00:17:25.040
"Come up to room whatever it is 6:15." He&nbsp;
comes to room 615. I've propped the door&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.040 --> 00:17:33.200
open. He comes in. I tackle him. He's trying to&nbsp;
get the gun out of his waistband. I have his arm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:38.400
his hand. My colleagues burst in from the side&nbsp;
and I'm just sitting on him. I have him pinned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:43.680
down on the floor and I'm sitting on his chest.&nbsp;
In the meantime, he's just like Allah Akbar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:54.640
Allah Akbar. And so my liaison partners gave him a&nbsp;
shot of demerol and knocked him out. We put him on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.640 --> 00:18:00.480
a gurney and covered him up like he was dead and&nbsp;
we put him in the service elevator, took him down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:06.080
put him in a an ambulance and we took him to&nbsp;
intelligence service headquarters. He woke up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:12.880
two hours later tied to a chair. I said, "We can&nbsp;
do it the easy way. We can do it the hard way. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.880 --> 00:18:23.520
totally up to you. we know who you are. He was the&nbsp;
leader of that country's Hezbollah wing. I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.520 --> 00:18:28.800
"We want all the weapons, all of them. We know&nbsp;
that you're the keeper of the weapons." He's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.800 --> 00:18:36.880
"Fuck you." So, I said to liaison, "We should just&nbsp;
break into his house. I mean, there's a there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:36.880 --> 00:18:41.680
a good chance all these weapons are just in his&nbsp;
house." And they said, "We can't. He's got a he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.680 --> 00:18:47.600
got a a live-in maid and she doesn't know anything&nbsp;
and she never ever leaves the house. And I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:52.320
"Okay, well, let's just declare a gas leak&nbsp;
in the neighborhood and then we'll evacuate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.320 --> 00:18:57.280
the cul-de-sac and then we'll just go in. Bring a&nbsp;
locks and picks team in and just pick the lock."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.280 --> 00:19:02.320
They said, "No, we can't do that. We don't have&nbsp;
we don't have gas lines in this country. We use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.320 --> 00:19:08.960
propane. Bring a propane truck. We'll leak the&nbsp;
propane. Then we'll just declare the emergency."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.960 --> 00:19:13.680
The fire department comes. They evacuate all of&nbsp;
the six houses. We break into his house and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:21.040
find a safe, like a double size gun safe. It was&nbsp;
enormous. The biggest gun safe I've ever seen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.040 --> 00:19:25.280
The weapons have to be in there. So, we bring&nbsp;
the locks and picks team in and they've got,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.280 --> 00:19:29.600
you know, the stethoscope on and they're&nbsp;
doing the tumbler and they crack the safe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:36.160
They open it and it's empty except for this&nbsp;
handwritten like childlike handwritten map.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.160 --> 00:19:42.160
So, we get in these two jeeps. We drive south as&nbsp;
the map tells us to do. We get out where it tells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.160 --> 00:19:53.920
us to get out. And we found this bunker full to&nbsp;
the to the ceiling with every weapon they had,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.920 --> 00:20:01.120
landmines, hand grenades, rocket propelled&nbsp;
grenades, everything they had. We completely put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.120 --> 00:20:12.080
Hezbollah out of business in that country. And as&nbsp;
it turns out, he's still in prison 25 years later.

00:20:12.080 --> 00:20:17.520
And SDR is a surveillance detection route. This&nbsp;
is something that literally every CIA officer must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.520 --> 00:20:24.320
be trained in. This is what's going to keep you&nbsp;
from being assassinated or arrested. And so you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:29.840
leave what's called your kickoff point. It could&nbsp;
be home, the office, wherever you happen to be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.840 --> 00:20:35.760
You'll do one that's maybe two hours. is if you're&nbsp;
in a very hostile place, your SDR could be eight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.760 --> 00:20:40.960
hours. You're changing into pre-positioned cars,&nbsp;
you're taking the subway, then you're walking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.960 --> 00:20:46.080
then you get in a different car, maybe you take&nbsp;
an Uber for a little bit of it. An SDR comes in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:52.400
four different phases. The kickoff phase, you just&nbsp;
go from from your kickoff to point A, and then you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.400 --> 00:20:59.040
do kind of a it's called a stair step. make a&nbsp;
right, a left, a right, a left, a right, a left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.040 --> 00:21:06.800
to go to your next stop, which is maybe, you know,&nbsp;
it's a fabric store. When you're stair-stepping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.800 --> 00:21:11.760
like that, you can definitely see if you're&nbsp;
being followed. If you're being followed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.760 --> 00:21:19.120
you abort the meeting and you just drive home&nbsp;
and you do the whole thing again 24 hours later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:19.120 --> 00:21:26.080
If you are not sure you're being followed,&nbsp;
you enter what's called the provocative phase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.080 --> 00:21:32.880
where you'll drive halfway up a block, then make a&nbsp;
U-turn and drive back. Make a K-turn, drive back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.880 --> 00:21:37.440
pull into another driveway, make a K-turn. Stuff&nbsp;
that doesn't make any sense at all. But that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.440 --> 00:21:45.440
just to make sure with 100% certainty that you're&nbsp;
not being followed. I served overseas with a with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.440 --> 00:21:50.880
a woman who was a legitimate State Department&nbsp;
officer. She came into the office one day and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.880 --> 00:21:57.040
she said, "Oh, I was under heavy surveillance&nbsp;
last night. I took them on a ride that they'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.040 --> 00:22:02.800
never forget. I was going 100 miles an hour down&nbsp;
the highway and I'm just sitting there listening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.800 --> 00:22:08.160
because what happened was they determined&nbsp;
that because she tried so hard to lose them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:15.520
she must be the CIA officer." And so they never&nbsp;
ever surveilled me because I'm going to the wine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.520 --> 00:22:19.440
store and going to the fabric store and then I'm&nbsp;
driving home. but they were plenty interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:19.440 --> 00:22:25.520
in her. You have to be very very careful not to&nbsp;
establish patterns and I almost got myself burned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:25.520 --> 00:22:31.680
twice. So there was one there was one point where&nbsp;
I was flying to Eastern Europe once a month to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.680 --> 00:22:38.400
handle an asset that I had recruited. He was very&nbsp;
proud of the village that he had come from. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.400 --> 00:22:43.200
high up in the mountains and there was a hunting&nbsp;
lodge up there. He said, "Let's go to this hunting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:43.200 --> 00:22:48.880
lodge. I remember it so fondly from my childhood.&nbsp;
And they serve wild boar and they serve bear stew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.880 --> 00:22:54.880
and all kinds of crazy stuff. So I went with him.&nbsp;
I ordered a very unusual drink which I shouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:54.880 --> 00:22:59.840
have but it was something my grandmother used to&nbsp;
drink and I remembered it from my childhood and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.840 --> 00:23:05.120
was talking about his childhood. So I ordered the&nbsp;
drink and the waitress brought it to me. The next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.120 --> 00:23:12.720
month I flew up to see him and he said, "Let's go&nbsp;
to the hunting lodge again. They have venison this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.720 --> 00:23:18.640
time of the year and it's really amazing. So,&nbsp;
we drive to Honey Lodge and I order the drink,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:18.640 --> 00:23:26.480
the same drink, and the waitress says, "I remember&nbsp;
you." And I said, "No, no, I don't think so. I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:26.480 --> 00:23:33.040
never been here before." And she said, "Yes, you&nbsp;
have. You're the only person who's ever ordered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:33.040 --> 00:23:38.400
that drink, and I've been here for 10 years." I&nbsp;
said, "Oh, that's right. I was here once before."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:38.400 --> 00:23:45.440
It's like, "Dog gone it." she spotted me. Now,&nbsp;
she was just a waitress, but what if she had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:45.440 --> 00:23:50.800
an intelligence officer posing as a waitress? Or&nbsp;
what if she was a police informant who's going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:50.800 --> 00:23:56.560
now rat me out to the local cops that I'm meeting&nbsp;
with this, you know, this guy? And so afterwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:56.560 --> 00:24:07.760
I said, "I am so sorry. I blew it. We can never&nbsp;
ever go back there again." And we never did.

00:24:07.760 --> 00:24:14.240
In January of 2002, I was named chief of CIA&nbsp;
counterterrorism operations in Pakistan and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:14.240 --> 00:24:19.840
there was a flood of al-Qaeda fighters across&nbsp;
the border into Pakistan. So I went out and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:19.840 --> 00:24:24.960
came up with this idea that instead of trying&nbsp;
to catch them in these forbidding mountains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:24.960 --> 00:24:31.280
we'll catch five, 10, 20 at a time in the safe&nbsp;
houses. So that's the practice that we adopted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:31.280 --> 00:24:37.680
And then after I had been in Pakistan about six&nbsp;
weeks, we got word that Abu Zubaydah was somewhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:37.680 --> 00:24:44.960
in Pakistan. We thought at the time wrongly that&nbsp;
Abu Zubaydah was the number three in al-Qaeda.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:44.960 --> 00:24:49.440
If you wanted to be smuggled into Afghanistan to&nbsp;
make jihad, he would smuggle you in. And finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:49.440 --> 00:24:55.040
I asked headquarters to send a friend of mine out.&nbsp;
He was a a targeting analyst, somebody who pours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:55.040 --> 00:25:02.640
through millions of bits of data to try to isolate&nbsp;
the location of one person. I said, "Buddy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:02.640 --> 00:25:07.200
we're on the trail of Abu Zubaydah." And I said,&nbsp;
"But he's right ahead of us. We just can't catch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:07.200 --> 00:25:13.280
him." Like sometimes we'd break down a door and&nbsp;
there's a cigarette that's still lit. So I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:13.280 --> 00:25:17.840
"You've got to you got to pin this down for me. We&nbsp;
just can't find him." Finally, he came to me and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:17.840 --> 00:25:23.040
he said, "I can't get it down to any fewer than&nbsp;
14 sites." I said, '14 sites. We've never done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:23.040 --> 00:25:29.040
more than two raids in a single night before.&nbsp;
Like, this doesn't really help. So, I cabled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:29.040 --> 00:25:35.920
headquarters and I said, "We're going to need&nbsp;
a big team." I asked for 36 people. I asked for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:35.920 --> 00:25:44.640
a pallet of weapons and ammunition and cash and&nbsp;
night vision goggles and more battering rams and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:44.640 --> 00:25:52.960
you name it. And then the next day, a colleague&nbsp;
of mine and two Pakistani officers started going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:52.960 --> 00:25:57.040
from sight to sight to look at all 14 sites. We&nbsp;
wanted to make sure we weren't driving into an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:57.040 --> 00:26:02.560
ambush. Do they know we're coming tonight? Most&nbsp;
of these places were little hovels, little one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:02.560 --> 00:26:09.120
room shacks made of concrete block or corrugated&nbsp;
tin. It was miserable. We come out of the back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:09.120 --> 00:26:16.880
end of the University of Faisalabad and there's&nbsp;
this enormous yellow house which corresponds with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:16.880 --> 00:26:22.720
site 12 and my Pakistani counterpart said I can&nbsp;
tell you right now something bad is happening in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:22.720 --> 00:26:29.200
there. He said it's 100° right now and all their&nbsp;
shutters are closed. They have to be broiling in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:29.200 --> 00:26:36.720
there but they want people to think there's&nbsp;
nobody home. We caught dozens and dozens of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:36.720 --> 00:26:43.920
people crammed into that house. We're going to&nbsp;
the last site and the analyst calls me and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:43.920 --> 00:26:51.520
says Abu Zubaydah just made a terrible mistake.&nbsp;
He accessed his emails from a landline. I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:51.520 --> 00:26:58.160
"Oh my god, and the landline is associated with&nbsp;
an address." And he says, "And it's site 13."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:58.160 --> 00:27:04.640
I still have chills thinking about it. And I&nbsp;
said, I we got him. So, we drive to site 13,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:04.640 --> 00:27:11.120
but it's an empty field. I said, how can this&nbsp;
be? We're sure that he's here, but there's no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:11.120 --> 00:27:15.840
house here. It's just an empty field. And&nbsp;
the Pakistani major laughs, and he says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:15.840 --> 00:27:22.240
now this happens all the time. He calls a young&nbsp;
technical officer, climbs the pole, goes through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:22.240 --> 00:27:29.200
this head of wires, finds the wire, follows&nbsp;
it down the pole, down the alley, and he says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:29.200 --> 00:27:38.320
"It's that house right there." We all gathered.&nbsp;
It was like 60, 70 of us, Pakistanis, FBI, CIA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:38.320 --> 00:27:45.360
we all gathered at the safe house. I stood on the&nbsp;
coffee table and I said, "Okay, so rule number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:45.360 --> 00:27:52.960
one, we have to take him alive." And exactly&nbsp;
as the clock strikes two, break down the door,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:52.960 --> 00:27:59.920
separate the women and children from the men and&nbsp;
take all the men to the Faisalabad jail. And so at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:59.920 --> 00:28:05.440
2 a.m. I was on the roof of the safe house with&nbsp;
my buddy. And I looked at my watch and I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:05.440 --> 00:28:13.280
"O200, here we go." And then we hear gunshots and&nbsp;
I said, "That's not good." We jump into our car.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:13.280 --> 00:28:22.160
We speed over to site 13 and there are three&nbsp;
people laying in the street. One is obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:22.160 --> 00:28:28.640
dead. One looks like he's dead or he's going to be&nbsp;
dead pretty soon. And the other one is screaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:28.640 --> 00:28:36.320
bloody murder. So I said, "What happened?" And&nbsp;
this Pakistani policeman says, "We got him. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:36.320 --> 00:28:43.280
got your man." and he points at the guy who's&nbsp;
like almost dead. I didn't have time to scream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:43.280 --> 00:28:49.120
at this guy that the order was to take him alive.&nbsp;
What happened was Abu Zubaydah, a Syrian bomber,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:49.120 --> 00:28:53.840
and Abu Zubaydah's bodyguard climbed to the roof&nbsp;
of the safe house and were jumping to the roof of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:53.840 --> 00:28:58.480
the neighboring house to try to escape. And this&nbsp;
Pakistani policeman was just picking them off as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:58.480 --> 00:29:03.440
they were jumping. And he shot Abu Zubaydah three&nbsp;
times in the thigh, the groin, and the stomach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:04.080 --> 00:29:10.240
So, I said, "This doesn't look anything like our&nbsp;
target." We had a six-year-old passport photo. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:10.240 --> 00:29:14.320
I called the analyst. I said, "Listen, I don't&nbsp;
know if it's him." He said, "Give me a shot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:14.320 --> 00:29:19.600
his eye. I'll run a retinal scan." So, I lifted up&nbsp;
his eye. And it was rolled back in his head. All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:19.600 --> 00:29:24.240
you could see was white. He's bleeding to death.&nbsp;
All I get are the whites of his eyes. He said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:24.240 --> 00:29:28.960
"Get me his ear. Take a picture of his ear."&nbsp;
I didn't know that no two people on Earth have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:28.960 --> 00:29:34.240
the same ears until that night. They're like&nbsp;
fingerprints. So I take a picture of the ear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:34.240 --> 00:29:40.160
headquarters says it's him. So we pick him up,&nbsp;
throw him in the back of this pickup truck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:40.160 --> 00:29:46.800
speed to Faisalabad Hospital. So they rush him&nbsp;
into surgery. But word got around the al-Qaeda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:46.800 --> 00:29:51.600
community that we had gotten him. And so they&nbsp;
started driving by the hospital and just opening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:51.600 --> 00:29:56.960
fire on the hospital. We kept diving down. I&nbsp;
said to the Pakistani major that I was with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:56.960 --> 00:30:03.680
"If they realize how lightly armed we are, we're&nbsp;
dead." I said, "Can you get a helicopter in here?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:03.680 --> 00:30:09.440
20 minutes later, a helicopter lands in the&nbsp;
parking lot. I walk into the to the operating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:09.440 --> 00:30:14.240
room with like this with my shirt up over my&nbsp;
nose. I said, "Doc, wrap it up. We have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:14.240 --> 00:30:19.200
go." We put him on the helicopter. Like four&nbsp;
of us got onto the helicopter and we flew to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:19.200 --> 00:30:25.520
a Pakistani military base about 50 miles away.&nbsp;
And the doctor there said he had never seen a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:25.520 --> 00:30:32.560
patient with wounds so severe who lived. But&nbsp;
he lived and sure enough it took a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:32.560 --> 00:30:43.360
while but Abu Zubaydah started to open up and&nbsp;
the information that he gave was actionable.

00:30:43.360 --> 00:30:50.320
The way we prepared for raids in Pakistan was&nbsp;
actually quite simple. I went online with my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:50.320 --> 00:30:56.160
CIA credit card and I bought everything I needed.&nbsp;
I brought battering rams and bulletproof vests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:56.160 --> 00:31:03.360
weapons and ammunition and walkie-talkies and you&nbsp;
name it. We didn't have gear that I think most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:03.360 --> 00:31:10.400
people would consider to be sophisticated. We all&nbsp;
had 9 mm, just 9 millimeters because they're so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:10.400 --> 00:31:17.600
reliable. We all had bulletproof vests. A couple&nbsp;
of the guys had helmets, not many. Lots and lots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:17.600 --> 00:31:26.240
and lots of ammunition. And that was pretty much&nbsp;
it. We got a tip with an address. We had two CIA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:26.240 --> 00:31:31.440
officers, two FBI agents, and two Pakistani&nbsp;
officials. And we went at two o'clock in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:31.440 --> 00:31:38.240
morning. Counted one, two, three, boom, with&nbsp;
a battering ram, bust the door down, throw in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:38.240 --> 00:31:43.680
a flashbang grenade just to scare everybody and&nbsp;
make them disoriented. And we grabbed these two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:43.680 --> 00:31:51.680
kids. They 18, 19 years old. They both burst into&nbsp;
tears. And so we got a tip later in the week from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:51.680 --> 00:31:59.840
a friendly Arab intelligence officer. So we did it&nbsp;
again and we caught some serious people the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:59.840 --> 00:32:06.240
time. A relatively senior member of Egyptian&nbsp;
Islamic Jihad and some al-Qaeda like mid-level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:06.240 --> 00:32:12.720
al-Qaeda fighters. We did it at 2 am because like&nbsp;
literally everybody in Pakistan sound asleep at 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:12.720 --> 00:32:16.960
am. It's like they just they just close the&nbsp;
country. There's no traffic. There's nobody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:16.960 --> 00:32:28.560
out there. Everybody's asleep. And so we figured&nbsp;
that was our best chance of not getting caught.

00:32:28.560 --> 00:32:34.800
I did dozens of interrogations when I was in&nbsp;
Pakistan and I was always the good cop. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:34.800 --> 00:32:40.160
was always easier for me, more natural. And the&nbsp;
Pakistanis because it was their country and their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:40.160 --> 00:32:44.720
rules are different than ours, they could be the&nbsp;
bad cop. So the very first interrogation I did was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:44.720 --> 00:32:51.040
of a Jordanian prisoner. They brought him in and&nbsp;
we chained him to an eyebolt in the in the table&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:51.040 --> 00:32:57.520
and I said, "I'm from the American government and&nbsp;
I want to ask you some questions." And he says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:57.520 --> 00:33:04.240
"Very well." Then I laid out a map of the Afghan&nbsp;
Pakistan border and I told him with his finger to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:04.240 --> 00:33:09.360
show me exactly how he crossed the border. We knew&nbsp;
how they were crossing the border. We called them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:09.360 --> 00:33:14.160
rat lines. We knew where the rat lines were. but&nbsp;
he told me the truth and I thought, you know what,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:14.160 --> 00:33:22.880
this actually works. And so we brought in the&nbsp;
next prisoner. Now, there were some that had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:22.880 --> 00:33:29.280
read the al-Qaeda training manual, not knowing&nbsp;
that I had also read it. And so, they're like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:29.280 --> 00:33:33.120
you know, they they pretend to pass out and&nbsp;
they just fall off the chair and you're just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:33.120 --> 00:33:36.560
sitting there and then like they're laying on&nbsp;
the ground, they open one eye and you're like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:36.560 --> 00:33:41.840
get back on the chair. What's the matter with you?&nbsp;
And then they get up back and sit on the chair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:41.840 --> 00:33:48.720
and then they oh they go oh like this stomach pain&nbsp;
and you're like buddy I read the al-Qaeda training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:48.720 --> 00:33:53.680
manual. I know all the different things you're&nbsp;
going to try to do. And then they just won't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:53.680 --> 00:33:59.200
answer any questions. So we send him back to the&nbsp;
jail. The ones that were cooperative for the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:59.200 --> 00:34:04.560
part ended up being released. Some were held for&nbsp;
a while in Pakistan. Then they were expelled, sent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:04.560 --> 00:34:15.920
back to their home country. But the ones who were&nbsp;
uncooperative from Pakistan went to Guantanamo.

00:34:15.920 --> 00:34:23.120
My conversations with Abu Zubaydah were generally&nbsp;
not substantive. After 56 hours, the CIA sent in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:23.120 --> 00:34:28.720
a private jet with a trauma surgeon from John's&nbsp;
Hopkins University Medical Center. He flew out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:28.720 --> 00:34:35.120
to the base. They picked up Abu Zubaydah and&nbsp;
they took him to the first of about a half a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:35.120 --> 00:34:39.360
dozen secret sites. They took him all over the&nbsp;
world just to make sure that the flight couldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:39.360 --> 00:34:45.840
be traced. So he arrived at the secret site and&nbsp;
was given six weeks to recover from his gunshot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:45.840 --> 00:34:54.080
wounds. And so after 6 weeks he was he was well&nbsp;
enough to be interrogated. So an FBI agent by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:54.080 --> 00:35:00.800
name of Ali Soufan began interrogating him. in&nbsp;
that FBI way where you treat people with respect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:00.800 --> 00:35:08.160
and establish this rapport. Maybe you offer him&nbsp;
a cigarette or an orange or a cup of tea or if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:08.160 --> 00:35:12.640
he's really really helpful, you let him write a&nbsp;
letter to his parents or whatever. And sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:12.640 --> 00:35:19.520
that takes days, weeks, months, whatever.&nbsp;
But they always open up. And sure enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:19.520 --> 00:35:25.360
it took a little while a number of weeks, but&nbsp;
Abu Zubaydah started to open up and everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:25.360 --> 00:35:33.360
that he gave Ali was true and it was actionable.&nbsp;
For example, we didn't have any idea what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:33.360 --> 00:35:40.080
al-Qaeda wiring diagram looked like. This sounds&nbsp;
nuts now, but in the immediate aftermath of 9/11,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:40.080 --> 00:35:45.600
we didn't know what this al-Qaeda really was. The&nbsp;
other thing he told us was even more important.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:45.600 --> 00:35:49.920
So Abu Zubaydah told us about Khalid&nbsp;
Sheikh Mohammed. We had never heard the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:49.920 --> 00:35:57.280
name before and we were able to focus all of our&nbsp;
counterterrorism assets just to find Khalid Shake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:57.280 --> 00:36:04.240
Muhammad and we found him. It took us another&nbsp;
year but we found him. 911 was still an open,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:04.240 --> 00:36:10.320
fresh criminal investigation. And so, you&nbsp;
know, the FBI needed to be interrogating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:10.320 --> 00:36:16.640
these prisoners. And the CIA resented that&nbsp;
we were overseas and the FBI was in charge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:16.640 --> 00:36:24.000
of the interrogation because the traditional&nbsp;
relationship is that the CIA is always in charge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:24.000 --> 00:36:38.720
overseas. The CIA insisted that it be them.&nbsp;
And so the CIA was not willing to be patient.

00:36:38.720 --> 00:36:44.080
They picked up Abu Zubaydah and they took him&nbsp;
to the first of about a half a dozen secret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:44.080 --> 00:36:51.440
sites where he underwent merciless torture. And&nbsp;
we have nothing to show for it. Torture is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:51.440 --> 00:36:58.400
worst possible interrogation technique. It does&nbsp;
not work. Yes. Eventually, the prisoner is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:58.400 --> 00:37:03.040
to tell you what you want to know or what he&nbsp;
thinks you want to hear. But there's so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:03.040 --> 00:37:08.560
garbage that's thrown in with that. It's going&nbsp;
to take your analysts months to pour through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:08.560 --> 00:37:13.920
information to see what's real, what's not real,&nbsp;
and by then, you know, the bomb has already gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:13.920 --> 00:37:19.760
off or the the operation has already taken place&nbsp;
and, you know, you've just cost more American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:19.760 --> 00:37:28.960
lives. Torture is not appropriate under any&nbsp;
circumstances. We caught a guy in Pakistan and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:28.960 --> 00:37:34.480
sent him off to be tortured. And I start getting&nbsp;
these hair on fire cables from the secret site&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:34.480 --> 00:37:40.720
saying, "Here's an address in Quetta. It's the big&nbsp;
al-Qaeda safe house. You have to go raid it." And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:40.720 --> 00:37:46.080
we bust down the door in the middle of the night&nbsp;
and it's just some old lady living in her house.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:46.720 --> 00:37:52.720
Then we get another hair on fire cable saying&nbsp;
there's a weapons cache and it's buried next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:52.720 --> 00:37:58.240
to this building in Peshawar and here's the&nbsp;
address and we go and it's it's a shish kebab&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:58.240 --> 00:38:03.440
restaurant and we've got you know metal detectors&nbsp;
and ground penetrating radar and there's nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:03.440 --> 00:38:11.680
buried there at all. The Senate Select Committee&nbsp;
on Intelligence Majority Staff decided to do a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:11.680 --> 00:38:18.800
complete and comprehensive study on the CIA's&nbsp;
torture program, which of course the CIA called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:18.800 --> 00:38:28.800
enhanced interrogation techniques. They ended up&nbsp;
writing a 5,000page report that is so damning and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:28.800 --> 00:38:38.880
so highly classified that all that was released&nbsp;
was a very heavily redacted copy of the 500page&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:38.880 --> 00:38:45.680
executive summary of the report. And so the CIA&nbsp;
essentially admits that it came up with a torture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:45.680 --> 00:38:51.840
program. It paid handsomely for the torture&nbsp;
program. It implemented the torture program. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:51.840 --> 00:38:58.160
I returned from Pakistan, I was asked by a CTC&nbsp;
colleague if I wanted to be, his words, certified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:58.160 --> 00:39:03.680
in the use of in the use of enhanced interrogation&nbsp;
techniques. I had never heard that term before. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:03.680 --> 00:39:08.720
I said, "That sounds like a torture program." He&nbsp;
said, "It's not a torture program. DOJ approved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:08.720 --> 00:39:12.880
it and the president signed it." I said, "This&nbsp;
is a torture program and I don't want anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:12.880 --> 00:39:17.360
to do with it." I was the only one who said&nbsp;
no. That conversation about torture bothered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:17.360 --> 00:39:24.800
me very deeply. And then I got passed over for&nbsp;
promotion. I just captured Abu Zubaydah with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:24.800 --> 00:39:28.880
these two hands. And we still thought at the time&nbsp;
he was the number three. And I got passed over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:28.880 --> 00:39:36.880
for promotion. I went into my boss's office. I was&nbsp;
screaming. And he said, "In your promotion panel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:36.880 --> 00:39:42.000
the director said that you displayed a shocking&nbsp;
lack of commitment to counterterrorism." I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:42.000 --> 00:39:47.280
"Because I won't torture people." Well, I was&nbsp;
also friendly with the deputy director of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:47.280 --> 00:39:54.400
CIA and he came up to me. He came up to me&nbsp;
one day and he said, he goes like this, "Your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:54.400 --> 00:40:00.640
name wasn't on the list." I said, "You believe&nbsp;
it?" And he promoted me with a stroke of a pen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:00.640 --> 00:40:07.680
which was incredibly generous. And then I&nbsp;
went on to my next assignment after that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:07.680 --> 00:40:14.320
I resigned from the CIA a year later, went&nbsp;
into the private sector, and I just, I thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:14.320 --> 00:40:20.560
you know, this torture program is so wrong and so&nbsp;
illegal. Somebody's going to say something to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:20.560 --> 00:40:30.080
media, and nobody did. And then in December of&nbsp;
2007, ABC News called me. I had never spoken to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:30.080 --> 00:40:35.840
a journalist before. He had a source who said that&nbsp;
I had tortured Abu Zubaydah. I said, "Absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:35.840 --> 00:40:42.880
untrue. I was the only person who was kind to Abu&nbsp;
Zubaydah. I'll give you your interview. And so we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:42.880 --> 00:40:50.240
met up at ABC News studios in Washington. I said&nbsp;
that the CIA was torturing its prisoners. I said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:50.240 --> 00:40:56.560
that torture was the official policy of the US&nbsp;
government. It was not the result of a rogue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:56.560 --> 00:41:01.760
And I said that the policy had been personally&nbsp;
approved by the president. I am very sorry to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:01.760 --> 00:41:06.800
tell you that Abu Zubaydah is still in Guantanamo&nbsp;
today. This is a man who has been in American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:06.800 --> 00:41:14.720
custody now for almost 24 years. He's never&nbsp;
been accused of a crime, let alone convicted of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:14.720 --> 00:41:23.840
one. And according to the Senate torture report,&nbsp;
quoting the CIA, he will never be freed. And when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:23.840 --> 00:41:31.120
he dies, he'll be cremated and his ashes thrown&nbsp;
into the Caribbean. It's un-American. I've been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:31.120 --> 00:41:40.880
in touch with his attorneys and they continue to&nbsp;
be slightly hopeful that the political tide will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:40.880 --> 00:41:46.720
eventually change and somebody in the White House&nbsp;
is going to do the right thing. I hope that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:46.720 --> 00:41:54.000
the case. I think he should have been released&nbsp;
many years ago when we first realized that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:54.000 --> 00:42:00.160
was not the number three in al-Qaeda, that he had&nbsp;
never committed a crime against Americans. He had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:00.160 --> 00:42:06.560
never joined al-Qaeda. And even if he had founded&nbsp;
the House of Martyrs safe house and the Kandahar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:06.560 --> 00:42:18.080
and Helmand training camps, he's paid for those&nbsp;
crimes many times over. He should be released.

00:42:18.080 --> 00:42:23.760
The CIA reported me to the FBI for revealing&nbsp;
classified information. They investigated me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:23.760 --> 00:42:28.080
for three more years. I had no idea&nbsp;
my emails were being intercepted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:28.080 --> 00:42:37.760
teams of FBI agents were conducting surveillance&nbsp;
on me. And then I was arrested in January of 2012&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:37.760 --> 00:42:43.520
and charged with five felonies, including three&nbsp;
counts of espionage. So, I was facing 45 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:43.520 --> 00:42:51.680
in prison. The government offered me 30 months,&nbsp;
of which I would do 23. And then the whole thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:51.680 --> 00:42:59.520
would go away. My lawyers told me that this could&nbsp;
be a blip in my life or it could be the defining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:59.520 --> 00:43:06.000
event in my life. Make it the blip. My wife and&nbsp;
I stayed up all night long and I said, "By God,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:06.000 --> 00:43:10.400
I haven't done anything wrong. I'm not going to&nbsp;
take it." The lawyers were so mad. They came to my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:10.400 --> 00:43:15.840
house 7:00 in the morning and he said, "You know&nbsp;
what your problem is? Your problem is you think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:15.840 --> 00:43:22.560
this is about justice and it's not about justice.&nbsp;
It's about mitigating damage. take the deal. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:22.560 --> 00:43:29.040
said, "If I don't take the deal and I'm convicted,&nbsp;
what am I realistically looking at?" And he said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:29.040 --> 00:43:37.120
"12 to 18 years. Take the deal." And so I took the&nbsp;
deal. The the sad irony, I would even call it the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:37.120 --> 00:43:44.160
sick irony of this whole story, is that I'm the&nbsp;
only person who had any connection whatsoever with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:44.160 --> 00:43:50.160
the CIA's torture program, who went to prison.&nbsp;
Not because I tortured anybody, but because I blew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:50.160 --> 00:43:56.480
the whistle on it. The people who came up with&nbsp;
the torture are free and rich, I might add. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:56.480 --> 00:44:02.240
people who approved the torture are free and rich.&nbsp;
The people who carried out the torture are free.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:02.240 --> 00:44:09.200
The people who covered up the torture are free. I&nbsp;
was the only one who went to prison. And honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:09.200 --> 00:44:19.120
I wouldn't change anything. I'm on the&nbsp;
right side of history, and they are not.

00:44:19.120 --> 00:44:26.640
I started working for the CIA on January the&nbsp;
7th, 1990. And my first job there was in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:26.640 --> 00:44:33.360
office. It was to be an analyst. I got bored with&nbsp;
analysis in 1997. And so I decided to make a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:33.360 --> 00:44:40.240
unusual switch to counterterrorism operations. A&nbsp;
counterterrorism ops position opened where they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:40.240 --> 00:44:45.920
were looking for an officer who spoke either&nbsp;
Greek or Arabic. It turned out that I was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:45.920 --> 00:44:51.280
only person in the entire CIA who spoke both&nbsp;
Greek and Arabic. They decided that it was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:51.280 --> 00:44:58.480
lot cheaper and a lot easier to take a linguist&nbsp;
and teach him operations. So I got the job. Then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:58.480 --> 00:45:05.440
you start the basic training on how to recruit&nbsp;
spies to steal secrets. And you go to training at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:05.440 --> 00:45:10.960
what's called the farm, which is the CIA's famed&nbsp;
training center. The first thing I did was a class&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:10.960 --> 00:45:19.600
that we called crash and bang. It was literally&nbsp;
crashing cars through roadblocks or having your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:19.600 --> 00:45:25.920
instructors shooting at your car with, you know,&nbsp;
paint pellets and you rolling out of the passenger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:25.920 --> 00:45:33.520
door and opening fire on a robot that's firing at&nbsp;
you from a van. The bang part was learning how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:33.520 --> 00:45:40.960
use weapons. the Glock 9 millimeter semi-automatic&nbsp;
handgun, the Smith and Wesson 38 revolver,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:40.960 --> 00:45:48.560
the Remington pump-action 12 gauge. We fired tens&nbsp;
of thousands of rounds and really became experts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:48.560 --> 00:45:54.400
in these things. It was a lot of fun. And then you&nbsp;
go into these other courses, things like defensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:54.400 --> 00:46:00.240
driving, counterterrorist driving, surveillance,&nbsp;
and surveillance detection. And then I went on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:00.240 --> 00:46:07.360
to my assignment in Greece. I was halfway into my&nbsp;
tour in Athens when I received a cable inviting me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:07.360 --> 00:46:15.760
to apply for acceptance to a new training course&nbsp;
called advanced counterterrorism operations and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:15.760 --> 00:46:25.520
jumped at it. So I flew back to the states took&nbsp;
six weeks and it was like in-depth high threat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:25.520 --> 00:46:33.840
high danger counterterrorism operations. Then we&nbsp;
started doing things like beacons for example or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:33.840 --> 00:46:42.080
listening devices or covert video or you know we&nbsp;
were just starting with this notion of drones.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:42.080 --> 00:46:48.320
It was all brand new. The driving portion&nbsp;
was more about crashing through roadblocks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:48.320 --> 00:46:56.480
crashing through cars. I got injured in that&nbsp;
training. I mean this is this is serious. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:56.480 --> 00:47:01.920
you're if you're in some island in the southern&nbsp;
Philippines and the al-Qaeda people down there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:01.920 --> 00:47:07.040
are trying to stop you and they've got two cars&nbsp;
blocking the road and you've got to crash through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:07.040 --> 00:47:11.760
you have to know which part of the car to crash&nbsp;
through. For example, if they're just like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:11.760 --> 00:47:15.440
right? The engine's too heavy. You're going to&nbsp;
kill yourself if you crash into the engine. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:15.440 --> 00:47:19.520
you crash through the trunk, it pushes the&nbsp;
car out of the way, and you are able to drive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:19.520 --> 00:47:24.640
off. You learn little things like the very first&nbsp;
thing I do when I get in my car and I still do it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:24.640 --> 00:47:29.680
to this day is I lock the doors before I even put&nbsp;
the key in the ignition. I lock the doors because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:29.680 --> 00:47:35.680
in training they have you blindfolded and then&nbsp;
they lift the blindfold off and you have three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:35.680 --> 00:47:40.320
seconds to respond to whatever it is you see&nbsp;
in front of you. It might be a roadblock. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:40.320 --> 00:47:45.520
might be a guy standing with an AK-47 in front&nbsp;
of you, or it might be a guy opening your door&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:45.520 --> 00:47:53.280
very quietly to put a 9 millimeter at your head&nbsp;
and say, "Bang, you're dead. You fail." And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:53.280 --> 00:47:56.960
you have to do the whole thing over again. So,&nbsp;
you learn all these different techniques. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:56.960 --> 00:48:03.360
sent us out to Utah and then Nevada and we just&nbsp;
drove through the desert over sand dunes and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:03.360 --> 00:48:09.360
you know, how not to get stuck. Then we were on&nbsp;
a track, you know, 60 miles an hour backwards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:09.360 --> 00:48:15.040
and spinning around. We learned how to do pit&nbsp;
maneuvers, but it's a lot of hands-on stuff.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:15.040 --> 00:48:19.840
You you're pretty banged up. Everybody had&nbsp;
whiplash when we finished. And I had to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:19.840 --> 00:48:28.320
my right knee replaced what we do for our country.&nbsp;
So they prepared me for everything. In retrospect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:28.320 --> 00:48:35.840
nothing could possibly have better prepared me&nbsp;
for 9/11 in Pakistan. I had been in Greece for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:35.840 --> 00:48:44.640
a couple of months and I had bought a 50-inch&nbsp;
TV. So, I had the TV on the back seat of my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:45.200 --> 00:48:53.920
BMW 540, my armored BMW. I noticed that there was&nbsp;
a motorcyclist to my right and he was trying very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:53.920 --> 00:48:59.760
hard to stay in my blind spot. When I slow down,&nbsp;
he slows down. When I speed up, he speeds up. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:59.760 --> 00:49:08.800
a little nervous. So, I unzipped my fanny pack so&nbsp;
that my gun was easier to get and I slowed down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:08.800 --> 00:49:15.280
He slowed down. I decided to slam on the brakes&nbsp;
and try to hit the guy. But he was good. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:15.280 --> 00:49:20.000
I hit the brakes. I swerve to the right. He&nbsp;
swerves to the right. I miss him. I speed up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:20.000 --> 00:49:26.320
He speeds up. I swerve to hit him a couple of&nbsp;
times again and I couldn't hit him. But then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:26.320 --> 00:49:33.600
it's Athens, which is famous for its traffic.&nbsp;
So, we come to this intersection. It's like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:33.600 --> 00:49:41.360
a major intersection. It's 12 lanes, if you can&nbsp;
imagine. And it's just jammed full of traffic. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:41.360 --> 00:49:49.120
I'm stuck. I pull out my gun. I'm starting to&nbsp;
kind of panic inside myself, not thinking. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:49.120 --> 00:49:53.360
in an armored car. Just crash the cars out of&nbsp;
out of the way. That's what you're trained to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:53.360 --> 00:50:00.320
do. But I'm going to like I'm prepared to get out&nbsp;
and shoot the guy. He gets off the motorcycle. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:00.320 --> 00:50:05.280
reaches into his waistband and he pulls out what&nbsp;
looked to me like a 38. It was a smallal-ish gun.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:05.280 --> 00:50:11.760
It may have been a 22, but it looked like a&nbsp;
38. I saw a path to escape. And so I gunned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:11.760 --> 00:50:19.360
it. I crashed the motorcycle. The motorcycle&nbsp;
just goes flying and I take off up GCS. So I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:19.360 --> 00:50:25.840
got to the cops and they found the motorcycle.&nbsp;
It turned out the license plate had been stolen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:25.840 --> 00:50:31.040
Then the question is, was this a an attempted&nbsp;
terrorist attack? We went out to literally every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:31.040 --> 00:50:36.960
single one of our counterterrorism sources.&nbsp;
None of them knew anything. And in the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:36.960 --> 00:50:40.720
and it was a weeks long investigation, they&nbsp;
determined that it was it was an attempted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:40.720 --> 00:50:50.880
carjacking. It was a thief who saw this at the&nbsp;
time state-of-the-art gigantic TV on the back of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:50.880 --> 00:51:02.480
a of a BMW. And he thought, I'm going to carjack&nbsp;
this guy and take his car and his TV. But it also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:02.480 --> 00:51:10.800
put me in a frame of mind to be more wary. And so&nbsp;
when I got to Pakistan, I was much quicker to act.

00:51:16.160 --> 00:51:20.960
I really believe that the future of&nbsp;
counterterrorism is a combination of human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:20.960 --> 00:51:27.680
intelligence and drones to infiltrate terrorist&nbsp;
groups. Though the drone's not going to collect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:27.680 --> 00:51:33.840
the information. You know, these bad guys, they&nbsp;
exercise electronic discipline. They're not going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:33.840 --> 00:51:39.920
to say the plans for the next terrorist attack are&nbsp;
going to be this, you know, on the phone. You need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:39.920 --> 00:51:45.200
human beings inside these groups to figure out&nbsp;
what exactly they're planning to do. But then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:45.200 --> 00:51:52.560
when it comes time to confront them, I think we're&nbsp;
very quickly getting to a point where you're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:52.560 --> 00:51:58.880
going to see CIA officers on the ground with M4s&nbsp;
and bulletproof vests and helmets parachuting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:58.880 --> 00:52:05.600
out of planes. It's going to be drones with&nbsp;
Hellfire missiles attached to their undersides.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:05.600 --> 00:52:14.720
I'm worried about AI vis-a-vis counterterrorism.&nbsp;
First of all, it's tough enough as it is to cross&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:14.720 --> 00:52:20.400
borders with six different identities, right?&nbsp;
How in the world are CIA officers going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:20.400 --> 00:52:27.520
cross borders or get through airports with&nbsp;
facial recognition scans and AI, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:27.520 --> 00:52:34.480
following their every movement around the world.&nbsp;
At the same time, AI makes the bad guys smarter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:34.480 --> 00:52:40.560
So CIA's got the money and they have access to the&nbsp;
right people and the right companies and the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:40.560 --> 00:52:46.000
experts to stay up on this. But this is this is&nbsp;
going to be an uphill struggle. The international&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:46.000 --> 00:52:53.840
terrorism landscape changes constantly. There&nbsp;
are some constants in that landscape like ISIS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:53.840 --> 00:53:00.480
or any of its variations. And then there are&nbsp;
both al-Qaeda and ISIS groups that have popped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:00.480 --> 00:53:09.600
up individually in northern Pakistan, in India,&nbsp;
in the Philippines, all over the place. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:09.600 --> 00:53:15.120
like playing whack-a-ole where just as you destroy&nbsp;
one, another one pops up that you have to bash in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:15.120 --> 00:53:19.600
the head, too. I think we should be choosing our&nbsp;
battles carefully. We shouldn't be casting such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:19.600 --> 00:53:27.920
a wide net. There's something recent that that&nbsp;
bothers me greatly and that is the government's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:27.920 --> 00:53:33.760
decision to expand the definition of a terrorist&nbsp;
group. Right? There's a pretty easy definition of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:33.760 --> 00:53:42.160
terrorism. It is using violence to put forward&nbsp;
a political goal. They're adding groups to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:42.160 --> 00:53:47.600
terrorism watch list that have never been on the&nbsp;
terrorism watch list any before. groups that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:47.600 --> 00:53:54.000
loosely associated with what is called Antifa&nbsp;
being added to the list is just silly to me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:54.000 --> 00:53:59.360
First of all, Antifa is not a group. It's an idea.&nbsp;
It doesn't have a headquarters. They don't issue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:59.360 --> 00:54:07.840
membership cards. They're just loosely affiliated&nbsp;
people who are anti-fascist. I would posit that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:07.840 --> 00:54:14.560
every soldier and seller who participated in D-Day&nbsp;
was anti-fascist. They would be Antifa. So, that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:14.560 --> 00:54:24.560
just silly to me. To me, that's a mistake because&nbsp;
it diverts attention and it diverts finances. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:24.560 --> 00:54:29.840
should be focused on the groups that mean us harm,&nbsp;
real harm. We should be out there trying to hunt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:29.840 --> 00:54:38.080
down the people who have taken an oath to kill&nbsp;
Americans. So, I'm worried that that it's too easy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:38.080 --> 00:54:45.440
now to describe people as terrorists. I disagree&nbsp;
with President Trump's decision to declare the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:45.440 --> 00:54:51.040
narco groups in Mexico and elsewhere in Latin&nbsp;
America as terrorist groups. At first, I thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:51.040 --> 00:54:57.840
that he did that solely for legalistic reasons&nbsp;
because it would it would serve to free up budgets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:57.840 --> 00:55:06.880
and it would also authorize NSA to begin wiretaps&nbsp;
or interceptions of the cartels communications,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:06.880 --> 00:55:13.440
which is awesome, great. but declaring them&nbsp;
terrorist groups other than them allowing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:13.440 --> 00:55:23.120
Pentagon to drone their boats, which may or may&nbsp;
not be their boats. I think it's misguided. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:23.120 --> 00:55:29.840
more of a civil libertarian in this respect in&nbsp;
that if these people are so dangerous and have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:29.840 --> 00:55:36.320
committed such terrible crimes against the United&nbsp;
States, charge them with a crime. You can't just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:36.320 --> 00:55:41.680
not like somebody's face so you fly around the&nbsp;
world to kill them. We're a nation of laws and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:41.680 --> 00:55:47.600
should abide by those laws. So Anwar Al-Aulaqi,&nbsp;
for example, the spokesman for al-Qaeda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:47.600 --> 00:55:54.000
guy's an American citizen, born and raised in New&nbsp;
Mexico, self-radicalizes, joins al-Qaeda as their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:54.000 --> 00:55:59.040
spokesman. We blew him up with a drone. He had&nbsp;
never been charged with any crime. If he was as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:59.040 --> 00:56:07.680
bad as you say he was, you at the CIA, then have&nbsp;
the Justice Department file a charge against him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:07.680 --> 00:56:12.160
But you can't just go around the world murdering&nbsp;
people whose politics you don't like and still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:12.160 --> 00:56:17.360
profess to be this beacon of, you know, respect&nbsp;
for human rights. I like to consider myself a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:17.360 --> 00:56:25.440
realist. If I had my way about it, I would tear&nbsp;
the CIA down to the bare studs and maybe rebuild&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:25.440 --> 00:56:31.600
it, but as a shell of what it is today. But I know&nbsp;
that's not going to happen. And so it's up to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:31.600 --> 00:56:38.560
rest of us through our elected officials to make&nbsp;
sure that the CIA remain law-abiding or become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:38.560 --> 00:56:45.360
law-abiding. Besides that, I often encourage&nbsp;
young people to apply to the CIA. You can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:45.360 --> 00:56:50.240
actually affect change from the inside. And I know&nbsp;
that's how if there's going to be change, that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:50.240 --> 00:56:54.960
how it's going to be. It's going to come from the&nbsp;
